<p>In the latest turn in the Iran war, several US allies — including Germany, Italy, Spain, the UK, Japan, and Australia — have declined or signaled no immediate plan to send warships to help reopen the Strait of Hormuz after President Trump urged countries that benefit from the route to join a naval effort. European leaders said they did not want to be pulled into a wider conflict and argued there was no NATO, EU, or UN mandate for such a mission, while the EU’s foreign policy chief said Europe had no appetite to expand its naval role there. The push comes as the US-Israeli war on Iran enters its third week and the strait remains heavily disrupted or effectively closed, with Iran using drones and naval mines and restricting passage for vessels from countries it considers hostile, according to multiple reports. Because roughly one-fifth of the world’s oil and liquefied natural gas moves through the waterway, the disruption has pushed up energy prices and revived concerns about inflation and broader economic strain. Even as tensions across the Atlantic sharpened, some governments pointed to options short of sending warships. The UK said it was discussing possible use of mine-hunting drones already in the region, and EU and UN officials were reported to be exploring a Black Sea-style diplomatic and logistical initiative, offering at least a narrow opening for de-escalation and safer shipping if talks move forward.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/xSGK48jdlz6isNusv3OA1hsXhp0NcPp4ImBTksHNHySrjoeDanUbBRiC7KVtwptT6wAxeA0QRu-Scbjxe_oRC_8iD-dpFYwMfpewBA8GNYmd' alt='US President Donald Trump speaks during a lunch with the Kennedy Center board members in the East Room of the White House in Washington, D.C., on March 16, 2026.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Trump warning: President Trump told the Financial Times that if allies gave no response or a negative response to his call for help in Hormuz, it would be “very bad” for NATO’s future.</li><li>German military calculus: Defense Minister Boris Pistorius asked what “a handful” of European frigates could add that the US Navy could not already do in Hormuz, arguing that more European ships would not materially change the situation.</li><li>Coalition gap beyond Europe: Japan and Australia also signaled no immediate plan to send ships, showing that Trump’s difficulty building a Hormuz coalition reaches beyond NATO Europe.</li><li>Asian dependence: Most of the oil moving through Hormuz goes to Asia, a point that stood out as Trump pressed countries such as China, Japan, and South Korea to contribute naval assets.</li><li>Consultation issue: German officials said Washington and Israel had not consulted Berlin before launching the war, which they cited as part of the reason Germany saw no basis for military involvement.</li></ul><blockquote>This is not our war. - Boris Pistorius</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>United States: President Trump framed the Hormuz request as a test of whether oil-importing partners would stand with Washington when their own trade routes were on the line. (<a href='https://www.nytimes.com/2026/03/16/world/middleeast/trump-strait-of-hormuz-warships-australia-germany.html'>The New York Times</a>)</li><li>Germany: Berlin stressed that no one had consulted it before the war, and that Germany’s Basic Law requires a UN, EU, or NATO mandate, making the dispute as much about legal process as alliance politics. (<a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982742/trump-upset-as-key-us-partners-shun-call-for-hormuz-warship-escorts'>Dawn</a>, <a href='https://www.csmonitor.com/USA/Foreign-Policy/2026/0316/trump-iran-war-oil-strait-of-hormuz'>The Christian Science Monitor</a>)</li><li>United Kingdom: London tried to leave room for limited technical help while resisting escalation, with Keir Starmer calling the choice difficult but saying Britain would not be drawn into the wider war. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/16/starmer-distances-uk-from-iran-war-as-eu-leaders-rule-out-sending-warships'>The Guardian</a>, <a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/video-uk-prime-minister-says-britain-will-not-be-drawn-into-the-wider-war-in/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li><li>European Union: Kaja Kallas described the EU’s preferred response as diplomatic and logistical rather than naval, including talks with the UN about a Black Sea-style shipping arrangement. (<a href='https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/eu-un-discussing-black-sea-type-initiative-for-strait-of-hormuz-kaja-kallas/article70749513.ece'>The Hindu</a>, <a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/video/newsfeed/2026/3/17/eu-says-iran-is-not-europes-war'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>US Republican strategist Matthew Bartlett: Bartlett argued that even reluctant allies have a direct economic stake in keeping the passage open because shipping disruption would ripple through energy and commercial markets. (<a href='https://www.rferl.org/a/coalition-protect-shipping-allies-bartlett-matthew/33708176.html'>Radio Free Europe/Radio Liberty</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-trump-laments-lack-of-support-from-allies-to-secure-hormuz/live-76387955'>Iran war: Trump laments lack of support from allies to secure Hormuz</a> - dw.com</li><li><a href='https://www.dawn.com/news/1982742/trump-upset-as-key-us-partners-shun-call-for-hormuz-warship-escorts'>Trump upset as key US partners shun call for Hormuz warship escorts</a> - dawn.com</li><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/635249-trump-world-demand-iran-hormuz/'>‘Not our war’: How the world has reacted to Trump’s call to patrol the Hormuz Strait</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/03/16/germany-rejects-sending-ships-to-hormuz-as-the-eu-seeks-a-diplomatic-and-logistical-way-forward'>Germany rejects sending ships to Hormuz as the EU seeks a diplomatic and logistical way forward</a> - mercopress.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/16/st